C++20 iterator concepts evaluated over a user-defined *class* iterator: the deep
concept chain random_access_iterator -> bidirectional -> forward -> input ->
input_or_output_iterator, which descends through semiregular -> copyable ->
movable -> swappable -> assignable_from and totally_ordered /
sized_sentinel_for.  Depends on requirement-parameter binding for conjoined
requires-expressions ([expr.prim.req.general]/2).  Includes the negative case
(a non-incrementable type is not an iterator).  See
doc/architectural/cpp-requires-expression-support.md.

Was KNOWNBUG since round 152 (main() partially type-checked).  Fixed
2026-09-23 in layers: requirement-parameter binding (a function template's
parameters leaked into namespace std as a secondary lookup scope);
common_reference over differing references (conditional operator
cv/value-category rules, a private member typedef as nested-name-specifier,
[temp.deduct.type]/5.1 non-deduced contexts during partial-specialization
matching, inherited-member lookup after on-demand completion);
same-pattern constrained partial specializations kept distinct
(__iter_concept_impl); is_base_of over indirect bases (the iterator tag
hierarchy); and cv-unqualified pointer-arithmetic prvalues
(`{ __j + __n } -> same_as<_Iter>`).  The source's `p <=> o.p` had been
reformatted to `p <= > o.p`; it is guarded by clang-format off/on now.
contiguous_iterator<int*> holds too once __is_base_of completes its Derived
operand (ITER_TRAITS(int*) selected the wrong __iter_traits_impl before).
